<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "The Accumulator Pattern with Conditionals" and
     "Accumulating the Max Value". GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-accumulator-conditionals">
  <title>Loops with <c>if</c> Statements</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      Any code can go in a loop body, including an <c>if</c> statement.
      That lets the loop make a choice about each element:
      do something with this one, skip that one.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-cond-count">
    <title>Counting Only Some Elements</title>

    <p>
      This program counts how many scores are 70 or higher.
      The accumulator only goes up when the condition is <c>true</c>.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-cond-1">
      <title>Counting Passing Scores</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-cond-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let scores = [88, 52, 70, 95, 64, 79];
let passing = 0;

for (let i = 0; i &lt; scores.length; i++) {
  if (scores[i] &gt;= 70) {
    passing = passing + 1;
  }
}

console.log(passing + " students passed");
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      Look at the curly braces.
      The <c>if</c> statement's code block is inside the loop's code block.
      Each pass of the loop runs the <c>if</c> check once, on one score.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-cond-filter">
    <title>Keeping Only Some Elements</title>

    <p>
      Combine an <c>if</c> with an array accumulator, and you can make a new array
      that holds only the elements you want.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-cond-2">
      <title>Keeping Long Words</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-cond-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let words = ["cat", "elephant", "dog", "giraffe", "ox"];
let longWords = [];

for (let i = 0; i &lt; words.length; i++) {
  if (words[i].length &gt; 3) {
    longWords.push(words[i]);
  }
}

console.log(longWords);
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-cond-max">
    <title>Finding the Biggest Value</title>

    <p>
      To find the biggest number in an array, keep track of the <q>biggest so far</q>.
      Start it at the first element.
      Then check every element: if it is bigger than the biggest so far, it becomes the new biggest so far.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-cond-3">
      <title>Finding the Tallest</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-cond-3" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let heights = [61, 74, 58, 80, 67];
let tallest = heights[0];

for (let i = 0; i &lt; heights.length; i++) {
  if (heights[i] &gt; tallest) {
    tallest = heights[i];
  }
}

console.log(tallest);
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      Why start at <c>heights[0]</c> and not at 0?
      If every number in the array were negative, a start of 0 would be bigger than all of them,
      and the program would wrongly say the biggest value is 0.
      Starting at a real element of the array avoids that.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-accumulator-conditionals-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-cond-count" label="mc-cond-count">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this program print?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  let nums = [4, 9, 2, 7, 10, 3];
  let count = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; nums.length; i++) {
    if (nums[i] % 2 === 0) {
      count = count + 1;
    }
  }

  console.log(count);
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>3</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. 4, 2, and 10 are even.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>6</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. <c>count</c> only goes up when the condition is <c>true</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>16</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The program adds 1, not the element.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>2</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. There are more even numbers in the array than that.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-cond-smallest" label="mc-cond-smallest">
      <statement>
        <p>You want to change the <q>biggest value</q> program to find the <em>smallest</em> value instead. What else has to change, besides the variable name?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>The <c>&gt;</c> in the <c>if</c> condition becomes <c>&lt;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. Now an element replaces the smallest so far when it is smaller.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The loop counts down instead of up</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The order you visit the elements in does not matter. You still visit them all.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The starting value becomes 0</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. Starting at a real element still works, and 0 would be wrong for an array of positive numbers.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
